-- ============================================================================
-- PD7 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Production Networks, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: WHAT THE SOLVE HIDES. Everything here exists only
-- because the answer came out of an ITERATION: what the solve pinned, what its
-- residual could not see, what it does when it cannot converge, what it says
-- when it fails, what a second module carries that nothing compares against
-- it, and where a branch relation the contract accepts breaks a Jacobian the
-- contract never mentions. None of it is reachable from the answer alone,
-- which is what an Expert tier is for. The tier ends knowing which numbers in
-- a network answer are DETERMINED, which are the LAST ITERATE, and which are
-- neither.
--
-- THE ORACLE RECORDS NONE OF IT, AND THAT IS THE STARTING POINT.
-- oracle_network.py is a genuinely independent referee for the PHYSICS:
-- Gauss-Seidel with a bracketed bisection at each node, no Jacobian, no linear
-- algebra, not even the same iteration structure, taking 19, 42 and 48 sweeps
-- where the engine takes a handful of Newton steps. It publishes four clean
-- cases and records NO defects at all. So every finding in this tier is about
-- something no oracle touches: the oracle has no concept of a pinned node, its
-- convergence criterion is a pressure movement rather than a mass residual and
-- is not comparable with the engine's, its 4000 sweep cap is never reached so
-- there is no published case of a solve running out of iterations, it never
-- calls checkConservation, diagnose or propagateStreams, it has no linear case
-- of its own, and it does not import pipeSchedule.js at all. THE ORACLE'S
-- SILENCE IS THE FINDING, and this tier has to establish every one of these
-- for itself.
--
-- THE HEADLINE. THE REPORTED RESIDUAL CANNOT SEE ITS OWN ERROR, AND THE
-- MECHANISM IS ONE LINE. `normOf` is the maximum over the unknowns FILTERED to
-- exclude the pinned ones, and `converged` is tested against that norm, so a
-- pinned node is removed from the measurement by construction. On the teaching
-- network the engine returns converged true with a residual of 1.546141e-11
-- lb/d while checkConservation, on the same answer, reports a gap of
-- 345.000000 lb/d, 2.593853 percent of what the engine says was produced. The
-- gap is 2.231362e+13 times the residual. Every unknown node is at zero
-- imbalance except one, and the one that is not is the one the reported
-- residual cannot see. checkConservation is IN THE SAME FILE, its own header
-- calls it "the only check that catches a sign error in the assembly", and
-- solveNetwork never calls it. Two candidate fixes, both cheap: report a
-- pinned imbalance beside the residual, or refuse to set converged while any
-- pinned node's net is non-zero. The second is the honest one, because a
-- pinned node with a non-zero net is not a solved network.
--
-- AND THE MESSAGE ASSERTS A DIAGNOSIS IT HAS NOT CHECKED. The warning says the
-- node "carried nothing and nothing depended on its pressure, so it was left
-- where it sits", and that "That is what a shut-in well on a dead line looks
-- like". On the teaching case that well is producing 985.000000 lb/d and its
-- flowline is passing 640.000000. It is neither shut in nor on a dead line.
-- The sentence is the module telling the user the one story in which the
-- pinning is harmless, on a case where it is not, and naming the node's own
-- net imbalance in it would make the difference visible at no cost.
--
-- THE MODULE'S OWN GATE BUILDS THE HOLE AND LOOKS AWAY. Its pinning fixture is
-- a well whose inflow is a constant 2000 lb/d on a branch whose flow is a
-- constant 1000, it asserts ok, pinned and the warning text, and it never
-- looks at the 1000 lb/d of production that goes into that network and never
-- comes out under a reported residual of exactly zero. Behind it is a contract
-- gap: the header requires `wellInflow` to be monotone DECREASING in pressure,
-- and a well held to a facility allocation, a choke limit or a compressor slot
-- is monotone NON-increasing with a flat top, and the flat top is precisely
-- what makes its node pinnable.
--
-- THE SHARPEST RESULT IN THE TIER, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN "UNDETERMINED".
-- THE PINNING IS THE NON-SOLUTION, AND A CORRECT ANSWER EXISTS AND IS
-- REACHABLE. Change nothing but `initialPressures` on the teaching network and
-- the conservation gap comes back as 345.000000 lb/d, as 1625.000000 with the
-- capacity limited flowline running BACKWARDS at its limit, as 0.000000, and
-- as MINUS 640.000000 where the network delivers more than it says was
-- produced. Every one of those runs reports converged. Six of the seven runs
-- pin the node and every one of the six is mass imbalanced; the ONE run that
-- pins nothing is the one whose balance closes, and it closes because above
-- 1013.848652 psia the allocation stops binding, the inflow depends on
-- pressure again, and the node keeps its Jacobian row and is solved rather
-- than left. The well's inflow falls to its line capacity at 1182.577035 psia,
-- and that is where the engine lands from the 1200 psia start, to the last
-- digit printed, with the well delivering exactly what the line carries. THAT
-- IS THE SOLUTION. The shipped default starts every unknown at the separator
-- pressure, which is deep inside the flat top, so the default does not merely
-- pick one of several equally undetermined answers: IT STARTS INSIDE THE TRAP
-- AND STAYS THERE. A solver that re-solved the node from its own inflow once
-- the row went flat, or that started a flat-topped well above its allocation
-- crossing, would return the consistent answer with no change to the
-- convergence test at all.
--
-- AND THE QUALITY NUMBER MOVES AGAINST THE TRUTH, WHICH IS WORSE THAN BEING
-- BLIND. Across that same sweep the two runs that are 1625.000000 lb/d out
-- carry the SMALLEST reported residual on the table, 2.2737e-12, while the run
-- whose balance closes carries the largest, 1.8917e-10. A reader ranking those
-- seven by the number the solver reports about itself picks the two worst. The
-- residual is not a weak signal here, it is an INVERTED one, and a user cannot
-- compensate for that. Two of the runs also agree on the manifold pressure to
-- the last bit while disagreeing about what the system produced, so agreement
-- between two runs is not evidence either. For scale, a pure reordering of the
-- nodes array, which changes no physics at all, moves every unpinned node by
-- 1.1369e-13 psia and the pinned one by 5.4710e-9. Two different kinds of
-- number are wearing the same label in the same object.
--
-- THE COMMERCIAL FORM, BECAUSE IT IS THE ONE AN OWNER MEETS. A deferment
-- measured off a pinned well's REPORTED rate is overstated by exactly that
-- well's conservation gap. On the teaching network the survivors gain
-- 188.982841 lb/d when the pinned well is shut, so the deferment is 796.017159
-- lb/d measured against the 985.000000 the solve reports and 451.017159 lb/d
-- measured against the 640.000000 its flowline delivers. The difference is the
-- 345.000000 lb/d gap and nothing else, and nothing in the return marks which
-- wells are pinned.
--
-- THE SECOND MASS NOBODY COMPARES. `propagateStreams` carries a mass for every
-- branch that is supplied by the CALLER and is never compared with
-- `wellRates`, so whatever hole the solve left propagates straight into the
-- surface split: on the teaching network the pinned well's flowline, the north
-- bypass and the trunk each show the stream mass 345.000000 lb/d above the
-- solve's, and the separator is told it receives the whole of a well test on a
-- line the solve says passes only part of it. The general form is worse than
-- the specific one. Hand the same network well stream masses that are simply
-- wrong, by any factor, and the module propagates them with ok true and no
-- warning, at 0.80 and at 2.00 alike. And the component rates do not move at
-- all when the mass is wrong, because the split is by mass SHARE and the
-- shares are unchanged, so the oil the separator is told to expect is
-- identical at a doubled mass. One comparison against `wellRates` at the top
-- of the function would catch the caller's mistake and the solve's own hole
-- together.
--
-- TWO SIGN CONVENTIONS UNDER ONE WORD, AND THE COLUMN CARRIES TWO DISEASES.
-- `flows[b]` is signed from the drawn `from` to the drawn `to`;
-- `branchStreams[b].massLbD` is the stream along the SOLVED direction and is
-- always positive. A consumer differencing the two to check itself gets zero
-- on a branch that ran as drawn and TWICE the flow on one that ran backwards,
-- and would read the second as a conservation failure. IT DOES NOT GET ZERO
-- EVERYWHERE ELSE, and this was corrected after the build rather than
-- assumed: four of the eight teaching branches are zero, the crosslink shows
-- twice its own mass, which IS this convention, and three branches that ran
-- exactly as drawn show 345.000000 lb/d, which is the pinned well's mass hole
-- from a different finding. One column, two diseases, and the factor of two is
-- the only signature that separates them. Both conventions are defensible;
-- carrying both under the same word in the same result is not.
--
-- THE TOLERANCE IS NOT IN THE UNITS ITS OWN NAME GIVES. The constant is
-- DEFAULT_TOLERANCE_LB_D and its comment says Newton stops when the worst
-- nodal imbalance is below it, in lb/d. What the solver stops at is the
-- tolerance times a SCALE, and the scale is the largest SINGLE well inflow
-- evaluated at the sink pressure: 7883.717950 lb/d on the teaching network, so
-- the documented default of 1e-6 is in fact 7.883718e-3 lb/d, looser than the
-- name promises by that same factor. A relative criterion is the right thing
-- on a system that might move a million pounds a day, and that is not the
-- complaint. The name, the comment and the returned residual are all ABSOLUTE
-- and nothing tells the caller which criterion is in force. The scale being
-- the largest single well rather than the total means the effective criterion
-- TIGHTENS as wells are added, which is the opposite of what a relative scale
-- is for, and the published wells_fight ladder shows the ratio going 1.000000,
-- 1.687637, 2.385868 as the second and third wells arrive. One smaller thing
-- rides along: the FIRST convergence test, before the loop, is against the raw
-- tolerance and every test inside the loop is against the scaled target, so
-- one criterion is spelled two ways in one function and only one of the two
-- decides. Since the target is never smaller than the tolerance that can cost
-- a wasted Newton step and never a false convergence.
--
-- EVERY FAILURE COMES BACK ok, AND THE FAILURE MESSAGE PRINTS ZERO.
-- `buildNetwork` trains the caller to key on `ok` by refusing a stranded node,
-- a missing sink, a duplicate id and an unknown kind, each with ok false and a
-- reason. `solveNetwork` returns ok TRUE when the iteration cap is hit, when
-- the line search stalls and when it sits on a cusp it cannot resolve, and
-- returns ok false only on a singular Jacobian. At a cap of 1 the teaching
-- return carries a full set of pressures, flows and well rates that look
-- exactly like an answer, on a trunk 806.447063 lb/d against 12955.677151 and
-- a conservation gap of 16740.257197. And the message prints the number it
-- just failed on with `toFixed(3)`: at a cap of 9 the true residual is
-- 3.876838925407e-3 lb/d and the sentence says 0.004, at a cap of 10 it is
-- 3.214654498152e-8 and the sentence says 0.000, on the one line whose whole
-- job is to tell the reader how far off the answer is. A reader who takes that
-- at face value concludes the solve met its tolerance and the flag is wrong,
-- which is the exact opposite of the truth. toPrecision(3) or an exponential
-- format fixes it and changes no arithmetic.
--
-- A CONTINUOUS RELATION IS NOT ENOUGH FOR A DIFFERENCED JACOBIAN, AND THIS ONE
-- IS AN UNSTATED PRECONDITION RATHER THAN A BUG. The turbulent branch law
-- q = k sign(dp) sqrt(|dp|) is continuous and monotone, which is all the
-- contract asks for, and it has an INFINITE slope at dp = 0. The engine's step
-- is h = max(1e-3, |p| 1e-5), so when the pressure difference across a branch
-- is smaller than the step the two central-difference evaluations straddle
-- zero and the entry the solver uses is a chord across a square root, a
-- function of the node's own pressure rather than of the flow. Walking one leg
-- of a loop moves a branch towards dp = 0 and the iteration count climbs from
-- nine into the thirties, the solve stops converging entirely over a narrow
-- band of conductances while still returning ok true, and then recovers to
-- nine once the branch passes through. The header should say a branch relation
-- has to be DIFFERENTIABLE at zero difference, or the Jacobian should widen
-- its step when it detects a sign change across it.
--
-- AND A RANKING THAT FAILS OPEN, STATED AS THE FAMILY IT BELONGS TO RATHER
-- THAN AS A DEFECT. `diagnose`'s bottleneck intensity is the pressure drop
-- over the mass with only a guard at a billionth of a pound a day, so a leg
-- carrying a millionth of a pound a day with one psi across it scores a
-- million and wins every time, and it is the one leg nothing can be done
-- about. On every real case in the digest the ranking behaves exactly as its
-- header says, correctly declining to point at the trunk for doing its job,
-- which is why this is a note and not a defect. A relative floor, one part in
-- ten thousand of the largest branch mass, would close it.
--
-- THE ONE ANCHOR IN THE TIER, AND IT IS WHY THE TIER RETURNS TO THE LINEAR
-- CASE. Everything above is a statement about an iterate. The linear network's
-- closed form is the only answer in this course that is not: a weighted graph
-- Laplacian solved by elimination, sharing no code and no reasoning with
-- Newton. Newton against it on the published linear_star agrees exactly at two
-- of four nodes and to 5.7e-14 psia at the worst of the others, in two
-- iterations. A tier whose whole subject is that a solver's report on itself
-- cannot be trusted has to end holding one number that never needed one.
-- ============================================================================

insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
values ('network', 'advanced', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-a-node-nothing-depends-on","title":"A Node Nothing Depends On","lesson_keys":["l01-when-a-row-goes-flat","l02-what-pinning-does","l03-the-residual-that-cannot-see-its-error","l04-the-check-in-the-same-file","l05-a-message-that-asserts-a-diagnosis"]},{"key":"m02-two-masses-under-one-word","title":"Two Masses Under One Word","lesson_keys":["l01-the-stream-mass-nobody-compares","l02-two-sign-conventions-one-word","l03-what-the-separator-is-told","l04-handing-in-a-mass-that-is-wrong","l05-the-comparison-that-would-catch-it"]},{"key":"m03-the-answer-and-the-guess","title":"The Answer and the Guess","lesson_keys":["l01-an-answer-with-no-tolerance-in-it","l02-the-same-network-a-different-guess","l03-what-a-reordering-moves","l04-when-agreement-is-not-evidence","l05-which-numbers-are-reproducible"]},{"key":"m04-what-convergence-reports","title":"What Convergence Reports","lesson_keys":["l01-the-tolerance-is-not-a-mass","l02-the-scale-the-caller-never-sees","l03-every-failure-comes-back-ok","l04-a-message-that-prints-zero"]},{"key":"m05-cusps-and-rankings","title":"Cusps and Rankings","lesson_keys":["l01-continuous-is-not-differentiable","l02-a-branch-at-its-own-cusp","l03-a-ranking-that-fails-open","l04-what-a-contract-has-to-say"]},{"key":"m06-the-expert-reading","title":"The Expert Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'network' and tier = 'advanced';

insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 1, 'solveNetwork pins a node, drops it out of the linear system and names it in pinned. What condition about that node puts it there?', '["Its Jacobian row is entirely zero, so nothing that flows depends on its pressure at all and the solver has no direction in which to move it.", "Its pressure fell to the module floor of 14.7 psia, below which no node is allowed, so the solver holds it there and solves the rest of the system around it.", "Its own nodal imbalance was the worst on the network, so the solver freezes the node it could not satisfy and reports the remainder as converged.", "Two of its branches move together, which makes the elimination singular, and a singular row is held at whatever value it last carried."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gate''s own fixture is a well whose inflow is a constant 2000.000000 lb/d on a branch passing a constant 1000.000000 lb/d, so neither depends on any pressure, and it comes back pinned = w after 1 iteration.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 2, 'The published pinning fixture returns ok true, converged true, pinned w and residualLbD 0.000000e+0 after 1 iteration. What does checkConservation report on that same answer?', '["The same zero the solver reported, because both figures are built from the branch flows the iteration accepted at its last step.", "A gap of 2000.000000 lb/d, since a pinned node contributes nothing at all to the delivered total and its whole inflow is therefore lost.", "Produced 2000.000000 lb/d, delivered 1000.000000 lb/d, a gap of 1000.000000 lb/d and a relative of 0.500000000.", "It declines to run, because a pinned pressure is not a solved pressure and an audit taken against one would mean nothing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Half the production enters the network and never leaves, under a reported residual of exactly zero, and the gate asserts ok, pinned and the warning text on this case without once looking at the hole.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 3, 'buildNetwork turns back eleven malformed networks, each with ok false and one sentence naming the fault. What does solveNetwork do about a node it has to pin?', '["It comes back ok false with a message of the same shape, since a node nothing depends on is a drawing mistake in the family of a stranded node.", "It reports the pin in a warning and carries on with ok true.", "It takes the well out of the produced and delivered totals so the two stay consistent, and notes the removal in the warnings it returns.", "It holds that node at the delivery point pressure of 265.000000000 psia, which is the one pressure in the problem that was never iterated."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pin is a warning and not a refusal, so a caller keying on ok sees nothing, and on the teaching network AGBADA WEST that warning sits beside a checkConservation gap of 345.000000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 4, 'normOf takes its maximum over the unknown nodes with the pinned ones filtered out, and converged is tested against that maximum. What does that do to an imbalance carried by a pinned node?', '["It enters at a reduced weight, since a pinned node reaches the measurement only through the single branch attached to it.", "It enters on the following pass, because the filter is applied only to the first convergence test, the one taken before the loop begins.", "It is added back once the loop has ended, which is why the teaching network reports 1.546141e-11 lb/d instead of a clean zero at the end.", "It cannot enter the number the solver stops on, because the node was removed from the array before the maximum was taken."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The one node that is not converged is excluded before the question is put, so the worst imbalance over all seven unknowns is 345.000000000 lb/d while the engine reports 1.546141e-11 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 5, 'AGBADA WEST comes back converged true at a reported residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d, and the audit on that same answer reports a gap of 345.000000000 lb/d. How do the two numbers stand to each other?', '["They are one measurement at two precisions, because produced minus delivered is exactly what the nodal imbalances sum to across a network.", "The gap is 2.231362e+13 times the residual, and it stands at 2.593852900 percent of what the engine says the network had produced.", "The gap is the larger because it counts every branch at both of its ends, which is the double counting a nodal balance avoids by construction.", "The residual is a mass rate and the gap is a fraction, so neither can be set against the other without first restoring the scale."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Produced 13300.677150912 lb/d against delivered 12955.677150912 lb/d, so a reader who sizes the uncertainty in the trunk mass from the residual has adopted a number the gap exceeds by thirteen orders.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 6, 'AGBADA-9 alone on its flowline reports a residual of 0.0000e+0 lb/d with a gap of 0.000000e+0 lb/d. AGBADA-12 alone reports a residual of 0.0000e+0 lb/d with a gap of 3.450000e+2 lb/d. Which returned field tells them apart?', '["converged, which is true on the exact answer and comes back false on the one carrying the gap.", "The iteration count, 7 on one and 4 on the other, since the solve that stopped sooner is the one to distrust.", "pinned, which is empty on one answer and names t4 on the other, and it is the only field marking a pressure the solver stopped deciding.", "No field in the return does, which is the reason the audit has to be run by the caller on every answer the solver gives."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both report a residual of exactly zero and one of them is missing 345.000000000 lb/d, so the residual does not separate them and converged is true on both.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 7, 'checkConservation sits in the same file as the solver, and its own header calls it the only check that catches a sign error in the assembly. What is its relationship to solveNetwork?', '["solveNetwork never calls it, so nothing in the solve return is a check that was not computed by the iteration it would be checking.", "It runs at the close of every solve and its gap is what residualLbD carries, which is why the two agree on the published linear_star.", "It runs only where a node was pinned, which is why the warning and the gap are always seen together on the same answer.", "It is called once before the loop to establish the starting imbalance, and the solver stops when that opening figure has fallen far enough."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A solver that converged on a wrong residual function converges just as smugly as one that did not, which is why the audit has to be independent of the stopping test and why the caller is the one who has to run it.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 8, 'What does checkConservation decline to tell you about an answer it has just audited?', '["The size of the gap, because it reports only whether the network balanced inside the tolerance the solve was given.", "Whether the answer converged, which is why its gap has to be read beside residualLbD rather than on its own.", "Anything about a published golden, because it is written against the teaching network and refuses a case it did not solve itself.", "Which node lost the mass, since it returns one gap for the whole system and cannot separate a pinned node from a sign error in the assembly."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It also cannot be read as a tolerance: a gap of 9.890573e-8 lb/d on turbulent_tree is noise, and the same gap on a network moving a few hundred pounds a day would not be.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 9, 'Two cheap changes would make the teaching network''s hole visible from the solve return. Which of them is the honest one?', '["Printing the pinned imbalance beside the residual, because a caller shown 345.000000000 lb/d at t4 can then decide what to do about it.", "Raising the iteration cap above the module default of 200, because a pin is the solver giving up before it has finished the work.", "Refusing to set converged while any pinned node carries a nonzero net, because a pinned node with a nonzero net is not a solved network.", "Asking for a tolerance below 1e-12, since the reported residual is already 1.546141e-11 lb/d and a stricter target would force the node back into the measurement."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine already computes the pinned node''s 345.000000000 lb/d and carries it in the returned imbalance object, so neither fix costs an evaluation. Printing it is useful and refusing converged is correct.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 10, 'The warning says one node carried nothing and nothing depended on its pressure, so it was left where it sits, and that this is what a shut-in well on a dead line looks like. What is wrong with it on AGBADA WEST?', '["That well is producing 985.000000000 lb/d and its flowline is passing 640.000000000 lb/d, so it is neither shut in nor on a dead line.", "It names the wrong node, because the imbalance of 345.000000000 lb/d actually sits at the north manifold and not at the well.", "It should have been a refusal, since diagnose in the same module reports a dead leg on this network and the two returns disagree.", "Nothing is wrong with it. The first clause is a fact about the Jacobian row, and the second is the standard field reading that any engineer would give it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'diagnose, on the same answer and in the same module, reports dead legs as none. The reading function and the warning contradict each other, and only one of them looked.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 11, 'The published fixture prints that same warning sentence with w in place of the node name. What does the repetition tell you about the sentence?', '["It was written for that fixture and generalises badly to a teaching case where the well happens to still be producing.", "It is a template with a node id substituted into it, telling the one story in which a pin is harmless.", "It is generated from the node''s own imbalance, 1000.000000 lb/d there and 345.000000000 lb/d on the teaching network.", "It is one diagnosis reached twice independently, since both cases carry a well whose inflow does not depend on pressure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture''s well makes 2000.000000 lb/d over a line passing 1000.000000 lb/d, which is not a shut-in well either, and naming the node''s net would have separated a harmless pin from a lost well at no cost.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 12, 'Walk AGBADA-12''s allocation from 640 lb/d up to 660 lb/d on the teaching network. What changes in the returned object?', '["converged, which was true at 640 lb/d and comes back false at 660 lb/d once the line can no longer pass the allocation.", "The reported residual, which was 1.9133e-10 lb/d while the node was live and jumps to 6.5211e-10 lb/d as the pin takes hold.", "The flowline, which carried 640.000000 lb/d and rises to 660.000000 lb/d while the extra mass is held back at the manifold.", "The word pinned, which goes from none to t4, and a conservation gap of 20.000000 lb/d that no field in the return puts on display."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every row of that sweep reports converged true and the reported rate on every row is the allocation, so the crossing is not a change in difficulty and nothing in the return marks it except one word.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 13, 'Across the allocation sweep the conservation gap runs 0.000000, 20.000000, 160.000000, 345.000000 and 660.000000 lb/d. What do the reported residuals do alongside it?', '["They grow with it, which is how a pin announces itself to a caller reading nothing but the solve return.", "They do not track it at all, running 1.9133e-10, 6.5211e-10, 7.2305e-11, 1.5461e-11 and then 1.5007e-11 lb/d.", "They sit at exactly zero on every pinned row, because the pinned node is the only node left carrying an imbalance.", "They fall in proportion to the relative gap, which is 0.001541345 at an allocation of 660 lb/d and 0.048473535 at 1300 lb/d."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The relative gap is 0.001541345 at 660 lb/d and 0.048473535 at 1300 lb/d, and the reported residual is the smaller of the two at the second, so ranking those runs by residual reverses them.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 14, 'The module header requires wellInflow to be monotone decreasing in pressure. Which well fails that requirement while still passing a loose reading of the sentence?', '["A well whose Vogel curve is steep enough that a small rise in wellhead pressure takes most of its rate away.", "A well sitting at the module pressure floor of 14.7 psia, where the inflow relation is being evaluated outside the range it was fitted over.", "A well held to a facility allocation, a choke limit or a compressor slot, which is monotone non-increasing with a flat top.", "A well whose inflow is a constant, since a constant is not decreasing anywhere and the module has no way to evaluate one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The flat top is what zeroes the node''s Jacobian row, and nothing refuses such a well: AGBADA-12 is allocated 985 lb/d on a flowline capped at 640 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-a-node-nothing-depends-on', 15, 'On the published linear_star the engine stops at a reported residual of 7.2760e-12 lb/d and the audit reports a gap of 7.275958e-12 lb/d. Why do those agree there and not on the teaching network?', '["Nothing was pinned on that case, so the filter removed no node from the measurement and the residual is a statement about the whole network.", "The branches are linear, so the residual is exact rather than a difference of two large numbers, and the audit inherits that exactness.", "Newton took 2 iterations rather than 11, so there was no accumulated step error left for an audit to find on that case.", "It is a published golden and the gate holds the two routes to a relative 1e-12, which is what forces the pair together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'residualLbD measures how well the system the solver chose to solve was solved, and that is the same object as the network only when pinned is empty.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 1, 'A network result carries a mass for every branch twice over. Where does the second one come from?', '["wellStreams[id].massLbD is handed in by the caller with the oil, water and gas, and the propagation walks it along the solved directions beside wellRates[id], which is what the solve decided that well makes.", "The solver writes it during the line search as the mass the branch carried at the last accepted step, so it lags the final answer by one iteration.", "It is the same number reported in two units, mass along the branch and mass at the node it leaves, which differ only where a junction splits.", "diagnose recomputes it from each branch''s pressure difference and conductance, so it is a second route to the same quantity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One of the two came out of an iteration and one was typed in by whoever ran the well test, and the module puts both in one object under the same word.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 2, 'On AGBADA WEST exactly three branches show a 345.000000000 lb/d disagreement between the solve and the stream: the AGBADA-12 flowline, the north bypass and the trunk. Why those three?', '["They are the three branches the solve ran backwards, and a reversed branch carries its stream along the drawn sense while its flow is reported along the solved one, so the two disagree.", "They are the branches whose pressure difference is smallest, so the solve resolved them least well and the propagation inherited the error.", "They are the path out of the pinned node, and nothing between them adds or removes mass, so the shortfall enters at that well''s flowline and rides through unchanged.", "They are the three legs of the loop, where the split between two parallel paths is decided by pressures the propagation never sees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'AGBADA-12 is allocated 985 lb/d on a line that passes at most 640 lb/d, the solve pins the node and still reports 985.000000000 lb/d, and five of the eight branches agree exactly.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 3, 'Differencing flows against branchStreams mass on this network gives five zeros, three rows at 345.000000000 lb/d and one at 1179.729250341 lb/d. Which of those rows the sign convention actually caused?', '["All four nonzero rows, because a stream mass is unsigned and a flow is signed, so any branch not running as drawn shows the disagreement.", "The 1179.729250341 lb/d row alone, on the crosslink, and it is exactly twice the 589.864625170 lb/d that branch carries.", "The three rows at 345.000000000 lb/d, since those are the branches whose drawn arrow was taken from a sketch made before anybody solved the network.", "None of them, because both conventions are stated in the module header and a caller who reads it differences the two correctly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That factor of two is the fingerprint of one mass written twice under two conventions. A mass that is simply wrong does not double, and the crosslink is the only branch here that runs backwards.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 4, 'A consumer differences the two masses on all eight branches to check itself. What has it actually measured?', '["A conservation failure on four branches, which is the correct reading and the reason the check is worth running on every solve.", "The pressure drop across each branch expressed as a mass, which is what a bottleneck ranking uses.", "Numerical noise, since the largest entry is small against the 12955.677150912 lb/d the trunk carries.", "One column holding two unrelated failures, a caller mass nobody compared and a sign convention nobody reconciled, so a reader who calls the whole column a conservation failure will fix the wrong one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Three rows are a second mass the propagation was handed and never checked, and the 1179.729250341 lb/d row is one mass written twice, and the two have no common cause.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 5, 'Why is carrying both a signed flow and an unsigned stream mass in one result a mistake, when each convention on its own is defensible?', '["A signed flow is wrong on any branch that runs the way it was drawn, so one of the two conventions is always reporting a sign that cannot be right.", "The caller chooses between them by reading a field name, and both are in lb/d and both are called a mass.", "An unsigned mass cannot be added at a junction, so the propagation has to restore the sign from the pressures before it can split a stream at all.", "The two are computed at different points in the solve, so they describe the network at two different iterations and are never contemporaneous."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A signed flow is the only way a reader learns the drawing was wrong, and a stream mass is what a separator has to be told, so both belong in the return. Sharing one word is what breaks it.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 6, 'The separator on this network is told it receives oil 3455.000000000 stb/d, water 1722.000000000 stb/d and gas 3536.000000000 Mscf/d. What kind of numbers are those three?', '["The sums of the four well tests to the last digit, because component rates simply add along the solved directions and the propagation does nothing else to them.", "Rates rescaled by each branch''s share of the solved mass, so they carry into the separator arrival whatever error the solve happened to leave in the branch flows it reported.", "The tests corrected for the shortfall the pinned node left behind, which is exactly why those three components close cleanly while the mass standing beside them does not.", "Estimates good to the tolerance the solve was asked for, since every quantity downstream of an iteration inherits its residual."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That arithmetic is exact even on the crosslink, which the solve runs backwards at 589.864625170 lb/d carrying oil 153.950443808 stb/d, water 54.517959468 stb/d and gas 238.312923093 Mscf/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 7, 'The trunk on AGBADA WEST carries a water cut of 33.262507244 percent. The four wells on their own are at 11.239495798, 54.951600894, 26.151665485 and 77.263969171 percent. What does averaging those four give?', '["33.262507244 percent, because a plain average and a mass weighted one agree whenever every well on the list is producing into the same header.", "A number that is always too low, since the wettest well is the smallest contributor here and a plain average underweights what it brings.", "42.401682837 percent, which is wrong by 9.139175594 percentage points against the trunk''s own mass weighted cut.", "Nothing usable, because a water cut is dimensionless and the four cannot be combined by any arithmetic at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A water cut is a consequence of rates that added, never an input that averaged, and the error runs in the direction the rates are unequal rather than in a fixed one.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 8, 'The published stream fixture puts a big dry well and a small wet one on one trunk. What does averaging their two water cuts cost against the trunk''s own?', '["Nothing, because the fixture is a gate case chosen so that the two figures agree exactly, which means the arithmetic of the addition is what is being checked rather than any part of the physics.", "A trunk at 40000.000000 lb/d against a mass the two wells never sum to, which is the error the fixture exists to catch.", "The difference between the two well cuts, 10.000000 and 80.000000 percent, which is what the averaging discards.", "A trunk at 27.500000 percent against a plain average of 45.000000 percent, wrong by 17.500000 percentage points and a factor of 1.636364 on what a facility would be sized against."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The trunk carries oil 2900.000000 stb/d, water 1100.000000 stb/d and gas 1690.000000 Mscf/d, and its cut falls out of those sums rather than out of any ratio the wells were quoted at.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 9, 'Multiply every well stream mass on this network by 2.00 and propagate again. What comes back?', '["A refusal, since the propagation compares the mass it was handed against wellRates before it walks a single branch.", "A trunk stream mass of 26601.354302 lb/d against a solved trunk of 12955.677151 lb/d, with ok true and no warning of any kind.", "A trunk stream mass of 12955.677150912 lb/d, because the propagation normalises the handed masses to the solved trunk before splitting.", "A warning naming every well whose stream mass disagreed with the rate the solve gave it, with ok left true."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The solved trunk stays at 12955.677151 lb/d on every row of that sweep, and 0.80 comes back at 10640.541721 lb/d with the same verdict.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 10, 'In the mass multiplier sweep, what is true of the row at a multiplier of 1.00, the honest one?', '["The trunk stream still reads 13300.677151 lb/d against a solved trunk of 12955.677151 lb/d, and there is no multiplier on this network at which the two agree.", "The two masses agree exactly, which is what makes the other rows readable as errors.", "The propagation returns ok false, because the honest mass is the only one that can be checked.", "The trunk stream and the solved trunk agree to 345.000000000 lb/d, which is the tolerance the sweep was run at."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A caller handing in the truth is not the same thing as the solve delivering it, because the well itself was allocated 985 lb/d on a line passing 640 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 11, 'At a doubled stream mass the separator is still told oil 3455.000000000 stb/d, a difference of 0.0000e+0 stb/d from the honest run. Why does the oil not move?', '["The oil is clamped to the sum of the well tests, which the propagation stores separately and restores at the delivery point.", "The solve was not rerun, so every component rate in the result is left over from the previous call and was never recomputed.", "The split at a junction is by mass share, and the shares are unchanged when every mass scales together.", "Oil is carried in stb/d rather than lb/d, and the module refuses to scale a surface volume by a mass multiplier."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every ratio a reader would compute from those component rates is untouched as well, so the one number that moved by a factor of two is the one nobody reconciles.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 12, 'One comparison at the top of propagateStreams would close both holes. What would it report on the four wells of this network?', '["A disagreement at every well, because a handed mass and a solved rate are different quantities and never match each other exactly.", "Nothing at all, since the check would fire only on a caller error and every stream mass on this network was handed in honestly.", "A 1179.729250341 lb/d disagreement at the well feeding the crosslink, which is where the two conventions part company.", "A 345.000000000 lb/d disagreement at AGBADA-12 and clean passes at 6004.874117054, 2318.356346320 and 3992.446687538 lb/d."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The check compares wellStreams[id].massLbD against wellRates[id] at the door, before the propagation has walked a single branch, and the same line catches a caller who scales every mass by 2.00.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 13, 'propagateStreams does have a refusal path. What does it refuse, and what does that say about the mass it accepts?', '["It refuses a well whose stream mass disagrees with its solved rate, which is why the teaching network''s propagation comes back with a warning.", "It refuses solved flow directions that form a loop, and names the network as recirculating, so the difference is not capability but which condition somebody thought about.", "It refuses a branch running against its drawn arrow, since a stream cannot be walked along a direction the drawing denies, and the propagation has no way to reverse one for itself.", "It refuses nothing at all, which is why the recirculating case comes back ok true with the loop reported in the warnings."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The message tells the caller a gathering system does not recirculate and to check for a branch connected backwards, so the module can write a refusal when it has decided to.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 14, 'Which independent referee stands behind the stream masses in this result?', '["None. The bisection oracle never propagates a stream at all, so the comparison against wellRates has to be made by the caller or not at all.", "The oracle, which propagates the same 4 published cases and records no defects on any of them.", "checkConservation, which audits the stream masses against the delivery point totals.", "The engine gate, which asserts the trunk stream mass on the teaching network."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oracle is genuinely independent for the physics, Gauss-Seidel with a bracketed bisection at each node, and it never calls checkConservation, never ranks a bottleneck and never propagates a stream.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-masses-under-one-word', 15, 'You are handing a facility engineer the separator arrival for this network. Which of the four quantities needs a second number beside it?', '["The gas, 3536.000000000 Mscf/d, which is the only component the split can misroute at a junction.", "The water, 1722.000000000 stb/d, because the trunk water cut of 33.262507244 percent was never checked against a measurement.", "The oil, 3455.000000000 stb/d, since it is the quantity every allocation meeting argues about and the one the split is least sure of.", "The mass, 13300.677150912 lb/d, which needs the solved trunk of 12955.677150912 lb/d beside it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oil, water and gas are the tests added up and match by construction. The mass beside them carries a 345.000000000 lb/d shortfall the solve found and the propagation ignored.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 1, 'On the published linear_star the closed form and Newton land on 546.666666667 psia at w1 with a difference of 0.0000e+0 psia. What is that agreement evidence about?', '["Newton''s convergence on this class of problem, since a method that matches an exact route once will match it on a turbulent branch as well.", "The shared work, which is the assembly, the sign each incidence carried and how the fixed sink pressure was held out of the unknowns.", "The tolerance the pair was solved to, which the gate holds at a relative 1e-12 and which is therefore the accuracy of both routes.", "The physics of a linear resistor network, which is the one thing two numerical methods running on the same equations can confirm between them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both routes were going to produce a number regardless, so what agreement tests is what they share. A sign error would have to appear identically in two separately written routines.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 2, 'Newton reports converged true in 2 iterations on linear_star. Why is that count the number worth reading on this case?', '["It is the count the gate asserts, so any other value would mean the golden had been regenerated against a different engine build.", "It shows the line search never had to halve a step, which is the only condition under which a reported residual can be trusted as an error bar.", "It matches the 19 sweeps the bisection oracle needed, and two methods needing the same effort is what makes the comparison fair.", "Newton is exact on a linear system, so a large count would mean the Jacobian is wrong rather than that the problem is hard."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The reported residual is 7.2760e-12 lb/d with pinned none, and checkConservation on the same answer gives a gap of 7.275958e-12 lb/d, relative 1.779446e-16.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 3, 'Seven starting guesses on AGBADA WEST, all reporting converged. The 1200 psia start comes back with the pinned node at 1182.577035 psia. What is special about that row?', '["It is the solution: the well delivers 640.000000 lb/d, the line carries 640.000000 lb/d, and the conservation gap closes at 0.000000 lb/d.", "It is the row on which the solver gave up earliest of the seven, which is the reason its balance closes and the reason its trunk is the lowest figure on the whole table.", "It is the row whose start was furthest from the answer, so the line search had to halve enough times to land on a clean number.", "It is the only row on which the well was throttled back to what its flowline can pass, which is a repair rather than an answer."]'::jsonb, 0, 'AGBADA-12''s Vogel inflow falls to the line capacity at 1182.577035 psia, and the 1200 psia start returns that pressure to the last digit printed.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 4, 'Why can the node carrying AGBADA-12 sit nowhere but 1182.577035 psia if the network is to balance?', '["Because that is where the allocation stops binding, and a node whose allocation has stopped binding is no longer flat.", "Because the solver clamps a pinned node to the pressure at which its branch reaches capacity, which is what pinning is for.", "A well cannot push 985 lb/d through a line that passes 640, so its node pressure rises until its inflow matches what leaves.", "Because the separator at 265.000000000 psia and the line capacity of 640 lb/d together fix every pressure upstream of the trunk tee."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing was throttled. That is the physics of a Vogel well on a capacity limited line, and the allocation of 985 lb/d simply cannot be delivered.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 5, 'AGBADA-12 has two pressures worth naming: 1013.848652 psia and 1182.577035 psia. What happens at each?', '["The line reaches capacity at 1013.848652 psia and the well stops producing altogether at 1182.577035 psia.", "The allocation stops binding at 1013.848652 psia and the Vogel inflow falls to the line capacity at 1182.577035 psia.", "The solver pins the node at 1013.848652 psia and releases it again at 1182.577035 psia, which is why the higher start converges.", "The Vogel curve crosses the reservoir pressure of 1450 psia between them, which is what makes the flat top finite in the first place."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flat top of the allocation runs from zero up to 1013.848652 psia, which is why every start below it reports 985.000000 lb/d into a 640.000000 lb/d line.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 6, 'The engine default puts every unknown at the separator pressure. What does that mean for this network?', '["The solve begins above the pressure at which the inflow meets the line capacity, so it walks down onto the answer from the safe side.", "The solve begins at a pressure the module refuses, since 265.000000000 psia sits below the floor of 14.7 psia that no node is allowed under.", "The solve begins at the one pressure that is not an unknown, so the first Newton step is exact and every step after it is a refinement.", "The solve begins deep inside the flat top, on a node whose Jacobian row is already flat, and ends at 831.176262 psia reporting converged."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A correct answer exists and the solver reaches it from a 1200 psia start in 7 iterations, so the shipped guess is not failing on a hard problem, it is starting inside a trap.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 7, 'The 400, 600 and 831 psia starts all report converged and all lose 345.000000 lb/d. What do those three have in common?', '["Each leaves the pinned node below 1013.848652 psia, where the allocation still binds, so each reports 985.000000 lb/d into a line passing 640.000000 lb/d.", "Each stopped one Newton step short of the answer, which is why the shortfall is the same on all three.", "Each was started below the separator pressure of 265.000000000 psia, so the solver had to climb through the flat top.", "Each ran the flowline backwards at its limit, which is what puts the same 345.000000 lb/d on all three rows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two of them are worse still: the flowline runs backwards at its limit, the manifold falls to 710.131972 psia and the gap opens to 1625.000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 8, 'The 2000 psia start returns a conservation gap of -640.000000 lb/d. What is the network reporting?', '["A well producing 640.000000 lb/d into a line running backwards, which is what puts the minus sign on the gap.", "An audit that failed, since a negative gap is the sign checkConservation writes when it cannot reconcile the two totals at all.", "A well making nothing beside a line carrying 640.000000 lb/d, so more is delivered than is said to be produced.", "A well held at its allocation of 985 lb/d while the trunk delivers 12046.088615 lb/d, which is the shortfall carried to the delivery point."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every row of that sweep reports converged true, and this one has the reported well rate at 0.000000 lb/d while its flowline still carries 640.000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 9, 'A reader ranks the seven starting guesses by their reported residual and works down from the best. Which runs do they pick up first?', '["The 1200 psia start, at 1.8917e-10 lb/d, which is the one row whose balance actually closes.", "The 400 and 600 psia starts, at 2.2737e-12 lb/d and a gap of 1625.000000 lb/d each.", "The default, at 1.5461e-11 lb/d, which is the smallest residual on the table and the run every other one is compared against.", "The 1500 psia start, at 1.5461e-11 lb/d, which is the only row the reordering test leaves untouched."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The residual gets better as the answer gets worse across this sweep, so that ranking picks the two runs furthest from the solution first.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 10, 'The 400 psia and 600 psia starts agree on iterations, residual, flowline, reported rate, manifold, trunk and gap, and differ only in the pinned pressure. What does that prove?', '["That the solver reached the same answer twice, so the 200 psi difference in the pinned pressure is inside its own convergence tolerance.", "That the pinned node''s pressure is set by the line capacity of 640 lb/d rather than by the starting guess, which only affects the path.", "That two runs agreeing on the headline figures is the standard way to confirm a network answer, since a shared machinery cannot agree by accident.", "That the pinned entry is decoupled from every other number in the same object."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Same 7 iterations, same 2.2737e-12 lb/d, same 985.000000 lb/d reported rate, same 710.131972 psia manifold, same 1625.000000 lb/d gap, and a pinned pressure 200 psia apart.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 11, 'Reversing the nodes array changes no physics. The unpinned nodes move by at most 1.1369e-13 psia and the pinned one by 5.4710e-9 psia. What do those two magnitudes mean?', '["The first is the noise of a different elimination path on a well posed system and is evidence the solve is determined, and the second is the last accepted step landing somewhere else on a node the solver could never move deliberately.", "Both are rounding, and the larger one only looks different because the pinned node sits at a higher pressure than the junctions do.", "The first is the tolerance the solve was asked for and the second is the tolerance it actually met, which is the scaled target.", "The first is the error in the Jacobian and the second is the error in the line search, which is why one is four orders larger."]'::jsonb, 0, 'unknownIds is built from the nodes array, the Jacobian column order is that order and the dense solve pivots on it, so reordering changes which row is eliminated against which.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 12, 'A reader runs the reordering test, quotes 5.4710e-9 psia as the largest movement across all seven nodes and calls the answer stable. What is wrong with that reading?', '["The movement should have been quoted against the separator at 265.000000000 psia to make it relative rather than absolute.", "The test is not a stability test at all, because reversing the nodes array changes the Jacobian column order and therefore the problem itself.", "The scale is not the finding. One node moved on a change that moved nothing physical, and that same node moves hundreds of psia under a change of guess.", "The largest movement is 1.1369e-13 psia, and the larger figure belongs to a node the reordering never touched."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two rows print identically to twelve decimals and still carry a nonzero movement, so a reader comparing printed pressures learns nothing about which kind of number is which.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 13, 'Two runs of the same solver agree on the manifold to the last bit and disagree about what the system produced. Why is their agreement worth nothing?', '["They were run at different tolerances, so one of the two has stopped further from the answer and the agreement is coincidental.", "They share the assembly, the Jacobian, the convergence test and the filter, so they can only disagree where the input differed.", "They agree on a pressure and disagree on a mass, and a pressure and a mass are never comparable quantities in this module.", "One of them had a node pinned and the other did not, so only one of the two answers has a pinned entry to disagree about."]'::jsonb, 1, 'solveLinearNetwork and solveNetwork share no code and no reasoning, which is why their landing on 546.666666667 psia at w1 means something and two runs of one solver do not.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 14, 'A network result holds determined numbers, last iterates and numbers that are neither. Which pressures on the teaching network are determined?', '["Only the separator at 265.000000000 psia, because it is an input, since every other pressure on this network sits behind a turbulent branch and no closed form reaches it.", "Every unpinned pressure, since reversing the nodes array moves them by at most 1.1369e-13 psia.", "Every pressure the engine reports converged on, which is all seven unknowns plus the boundary.", "The four well pressures, because a Vogel inflow fixes each one once the header it pushes against is known."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A pressure is determined rather than iterated only where the branch relations were linear, which is the condition the published linear_star meets and this network does not.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-answer-and-the-guess', 15, 'Five of the seven runs leave the north manifold at 780.469728 psia and the trunk at 12955.677151 lb/d while reporting three different amounts produced. How can the pressures match while the mass does not?', '["The manifold and the trunk are junction quantities and the produced total is a well quantity, and the module never balances the two against each other.", "The five runs differ only in a pinned pressure, and a pressure has no effect on any mass anywhere in a network solve.", "The branch on the pinned node is saturated at its 640 lb/d capacity in all five, and a saturated branch carries the same mass whatever sits behind it.", "The propagation restores the manifold and the trunk to the values of the honest run before the result is returned to the caller."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rest of the network cannot feel where the pinned node was left, but the reported well rate can, because the inflow relation still evaluates at that pressure.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 1, 'The constant is named DEFAULT_TOLERANCE_LB_D, it is 0.000001, and the comment above it says Newton stops when the worst nodal imbalance is below this, in lb/d. What does the solver actually stop at?', '["That tolerance in lb/d, exactly as the constant is written, which is the reason the residual comes back in the same units the name gives it.", "The larger of that tolerance and the worst imbalance seen on the first pass, so a badly conditioned network is never held to it.", "That tolerance multiplied by a scale, so the criterion is relative while the name, the comment and the returned residual are all absolute.", "That tolerance divided by the number of unknown nodes, which is the usual way a maximum norm is made comparable across network sizes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A caller who reads the constant, the comment and residualLbD has been told three times that the criterion is an absolute mass rate, and all three are absolute while the criterion is not.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 2, 'What is the scale the solver multiplies the tolerance by?', '["The largest single well inflow evaluated at the sink pressure, which on the teaching network with the separator at 265 psia is 7883.717950413 lb/d.", "The total mass the network delivers at the boundary, which is what a relative criterion on a gathering system ought to be built from.", "The largest branch conductance on the system, which sets how much mass a single Newton step can move.", "The sum of every well inflow at the sink pressure, 17556.445788322 lb/d on this network."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The four wells would make 7883.717950413, 3125.903030303, 5561.824807605 and 985.000000000 lb/d if each were opened straight onto the separator pressure, and the scale takes only the largest.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 3, 'A caller leaves the tolerance at the module default of 0.000001 on the teaching network. What is the target Newton is really enforcing?', '["0.000001 lb/d, since the default is the one value the module documents and the scale is applied only to a tolerance the caller supplies.", "7.883718e-9 lb/d, which is the target that comes of asking for the tighter 1e-12 this network is otherwise solved with.", "A target that cannot be stated in lb/d at all, because the criterion is dimensionless once the scale has been divided out.", "7.883718e-3 lb/d, which is looser than both the constant''s own name and its own comment promise, by a factor of 7883.717950."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing in the return names the scale, the target or which of the two tests stopped the iteration, so a caller who wants the target has to evaluate every well inflow at the sink pressure again.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 4, 'Taking the largest single well rather than the total as the scale has a consequence a relative criterion is meant to avoid. What is it?', '["The criterion loosens without any limit on a large system, so a forty well network can be allowed to stop at an imbalance no facility could ever measure.", "The effective criterion tightens as wells are added, so every well joining after the biggest one holds the caller to a stricter standard.", "The criterion becomes undefined when two wells tie for the largest inflow, and the solver falls back to the raw tolerance.", "The criterion depends on the separator pressure, which is the one boundary condition a caller is most likely to change between runs."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the published wells_fight ladder the ratio of total inflow to scale runs 1.000000000, 1.687637489 and 2.385868167 as the second and third wells arrive.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 5, 'On the wells_fight ladder the scale is 4125.742011834 lb/d with one well on the header and 4125.742011834 lb/d with two. Why did it not move?', '["The two wells share a header, and the scale is evaluated at the header pressure, which the ladder holds fixed across the runs.", "The scale is cached from the first solve, so it moves only when the network is rebuilt rather than resolved.", "The second well to arrive is smaller than the one already on the header, and the scale keeps only the largest of them.", "The second well''s inflow at the sink pressure is identical to the first well''s, which is what the ladder was constructed to show."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The total moved from 4125.742011834 to 6962.756887867 lb/d over the same step, so the standard a caller is held to depends on which well happens to be biggest.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 6, 'What does the solve return refuse to carry about the criterion it just met?', '["The scale, the target and which of the two tests stopped the iteration, so nothing in the object says whether an absolute or a relative criterion was in force.", "The residual, which is reported only in the failure message and is absent from a converged return.", "The iteration count, which the module keeps internal because a count is not a statement about an answer.", "The tolerance that was asked for, which is why a caller cannot reconstruct the target from the returned object."]'::jsonb, 0, 'residualLbD comes back absolute, in lb/d, on a criterion that was relative, and at 1e-12 on this network the target is 7.883718e-9 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 7, 'One convergence criterion is spelled two different ways inside solveNetwork. Where, and what does the mismatch cost?', '["The pinned nodes are filtered out of the one spelling and left in the other, which is what lets a false convergence through on any network carrying a flat node.", "The first test before the loop is against the raw tolerance and every test inside it is against the scaled target, which can only cost a wasted Newton step.", "The failure message tests against the raw tolerance while converged tests against the scaled target, so the two fields can disagree about one and the same answer.", "The line search tests an improvement in the residual and the loop tests its size, so a stalled search can satisfy one and not the other."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The target is never smaller than the tolerance, so the mismatch can never declare a false convergence. Only one of the two spellings is the one that decides.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 8, 'Walking the tolerance from 1.0e-10 to 1.0e-8 leaves the answer identical, and so does walking it from 1.0e-6 to 1.0e-4. What does that repetition show?', '["That the solver caches its result and hands it back unchanged whenever the tolerance it is given is looser than the one it last managed to meet.", "That the two pairs bracket the scale, so within each pair the target is the same number and the runs are literally identical calls.", "That the answer is exact at both, since a repeated result across two tolerances is the standard test for a converged solve.", "That the criterion decides when to stop and never what the answer is, because the target loosened past a step the solver had already taken."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The iteration count falls from 11 at 1.0e-12 to 6 at 1.0e-1, and the reported residual rises with it to 4.6571e+2 lb/d, so what loosening buys is steps and what it costs is the answer.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 9, 'Cap the teaching network at 6 iterations and the conservation gap is 41.125860 lb/d. Cap it at 8 and the gap is 345.000012 lb/d. What does the pair show?', '["That the solve is diverging somewhere between the sixth and the eighth iteration, which is the reason the backtracking line search had to halve its step on the way through.", "That the cap of 6 row is the better answer of the two and should be preferred whenever a solve is stopped early.", "That the gap on the way to an answer is not a distance from it, since the reported residual falls from 4.657099e+2 to 2.201349e+0 lb/d across those two rows.", "That the gap is meaningless below 11 iterations, because checkConservation cannot be run on an answer that has not converged."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The converged answer at 11 iterations carries the same 345.000000 lb/d gap, so the smaller figure at 6 iterations is a solve passing through rather than a solve doing better.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 10, 'buildNetwork refuses eleven malformed networks with ok false and a reason. When does solveNetwork return ok false?', '["Only on a singular Jacobian, which it reports as a diagnosis about two or more nodes moving together rather than as a repair it made.", "When the iteration cap is reached, since a solve that ran out of steps has not produced an answer.", "When the line search stalls, which is the module''s one unrecoverable failure.", "When a node is pinned, because a pinned pressure is not a solved pressure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cap, a stalled line search and an unresolved cusp all come back ok true with a full set of pressures, flows and well rates, so the field buildNetwork trained the caller to read is not the field that separates them.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 11, 'Cap the teaching network at 1 iteration. What comes back?', '["An empty result under ok false, since a single Newton step cannot produce a pressure at every node of an eight branch network.", "A complete return under ok true, with the manifold at 267.202639 psia and the trunk at 806.447063 lb/d.", "The starting guess unchanged, because the first pass is a convergence test rather than a step and no pressure has moved yet.", "The converged answer with converged set false, since the solver keeps the best iterate it has seen and returns that on any early stop."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The converged answer puts that manifold at 780.469728 psia and that trunk at 12955.677151 lb/d, and nothing in the shape of the object says which of the two you are holding.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 12, 'At a cap of 9 the engine says the worst nodal imbalance is 0.004 lb/d. What is the residual it is describing?', '["3.214654498152e-8 lb/d, which is the residual at the following cap and the one the sentence rounds to zero.", "7.545876e+3 lb/d, which the same format prints faithfully at a cap of 1 and which shows the format is sound.", "0.004 lb/d exactly, since the message is generated from the same variable the convergence test reads.", "3.876838925407e-3 lb/d, formatted with toFixed(3)."]'::jsonb, 3, 'toPrecision(3) would have printed 0.00388 and an exponential format 3.8768e-3, and neither changes a single arithmetic operation in the solve.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 13, 'At a cap of 10 the sentence reads that the worst nodal imbalance is 0.000 lb/d, on a residual of 3.214654498152e-8 lb/d. What does a reader who trusts the number conclude?', '["That the solve is exact at the tenth iteration and the eleventh is the module confirming it, which is what Newton does on a linear system.", "That the imbalance is below the scaled target of 7.883718e-9 lb/d, which is the criterion that was actually in force on that run.", "That the solve met its tolerance and that converged came back false in error, which is the exact reverse of what actually happened.", "That the message is describing the pinned node''s own imbalance of 345.000000 lb/d rounded down, since that node is filtered from the norm."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The solve did not meet its tolerance, converged is right and the imbalance is real but too small for the format, so the reader distrusts the one half of the return that was honest.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 14, 'The same failure message prints 7545.876 lb/d faithfully at a cap of 1. Why is that not a defence of the format?', '["It tells the truth about answers so bad nobody needed the digits and it lies about answers close enough that the digits were the only thing left to read.", "It is faithful only because that residual happens to have three decimals, and a fourth would have been dropped there too.", "It is the one cap at which no node was pinned, so the message had no warning to append and the number stood alone.", "It is the residual of the starting guess rather than of an iterate, and a starting guess is never formatted by the failure path."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The message is at its most misleading exactly where a user is deciding whether to accept a result, which is the one place a failure sentence has to work.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-convergence-reports', 15, 'A script consuming this solver keys on converged rather than on ok. What does it still miss?', '["The iteration count, which is the only field that separates a converged solve from one that hit its cap.", "The conservation gap, which is 345.000000000 lb/d on the answer this network reports converged on.", "The singular Jacobian case, which comes back converged true because the elimination returned before the loop began.", "The pinned list, which is populated only on runs where converged is false and is therefore invisible to that script."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reading converged instead of ok is necessary and it is not sufficient, because the converged row still carries a gap of 2.593852900 percent of what the engine says was produced.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 1, 'The module header says a branch relation must be continuous and monotone decreasing in pTo. What property does the solver need that the header never asks for?', '["Boundedness, because an unbounded relation can send a Newton step past the module pressure floor of 14.7 psia in a single move.", "Differentiability, because the Jacobian is a numerical difference and a difference is an approximation to a slope rather than to a value.", "Invertibility, because the solver has to recover a pressure difference from a flow every single time it assembles a residual at one of its nodes.", "Strict monotonicity, because a relation that is merely monotone can be flat over an interval and a flat interval has no unique root."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Continuity is a statement about a function''s values and differentiability is a statement about its slope, and the contract has not asked for the thing the method consumes.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 2, 'The turbulent law is q = k sign(dp) sqrt(|dp|). How does it stand against the contract as written?', '["It violates the contract, because a square root is not monotone across a change of sign and the sign function makes it discontinuous at zero.", "It satisfies the contract only where the pressure difference exceeds the Jacobian step, and the module refuses a branch that falls below it.", "It satisfies the contract, being continuous everywhere and monotone everywhere, and its slope at zero pressure difference is infinite.", "It cannot be checked against the contract, because the relation is a callback the consumer supplies and the module never evaluates it twice at one node."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The header adds that a pipe relation built from a characteristic curve satisfies the contract by construction, which is exactly the relation that invites this case.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 3, 'What step does solveNetwork use to difference its Jacobian, and how big is it on the teaching network?', '["A step of max(1e-3, |p| * 1e-5), which at the junction pressures of AGBADA WEST works out at about 0.007805 psi.", "A fixed step of 1e-3 psi, chosen so it is never larger than the smallest pressure difference a gathering system carries.", "A step proportional to the branch conductance, so a stiff branch is differenced over a narrower interval than a slack one.", "A step equal to the scaled convergence target, so the Jacobian is never resolved finer than the criterion the solve stops on."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every Jacobian entry the solver uses is a chord over that width, and on a smooth branch a chord of that width is a derivative to many figures.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 4, 'A branch sits closer to zero pressure difference than the Jacobian step. What does the solver put in the Jacobian for it?', '["A zero, because the two central difference evaluations cancel across the cusp and the entry drops out of the row.", "The analytic slope, because the module falls back to a derivative it knows whenever a difference straddles a sign change.", "An infinity, which the elimination then treats as a singular row and reports with ok false and a diagnosis.", "A chord across a square root, roughly the conductance over the square root of the step, which is a function of the node''s own pressure rather than of the flow."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two evaluations straddle zero, so what comes out is the wrong thing entirely to put in a Jacobian: it depends on where the node sits rather than on what the branch carries.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 5, 'Of the eight branches on AGBADA WEST, the crosslink sits 152.883678 Jacobian steps from zero and the next nearest sits 4915.076169. What follows for the crosslink?', '["It is the branch that cost this solve its 11 iterations, which is why it is also the branch diagnose names as the bottleneck.", "It is still perfectly safe, and it is the only branch here whose Jacobian entry is worth a second look, on geometry rather than on anything the solve reported.", "It is inside the step, so its entry is a chord across the square root and the answer on this network rests on it.", "It is the branch the reordering test moves, since a branch near its cusp is the one an elimination order can reach."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The trunk sits at 54991.975344 steps and the AGBADA-2 flowline at 37923.780524, so the crosslink is two orders of magnitude closer than anything else on the system.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 6, 'Raise the loop leg conductance from 245 to 400 lb/d per root psi and nothing else on the network changes. What happens to the solve?', '["It converges at 245, 300 and 340, stops converging over a band from 360 to 400, and comes back in 9 iterations once the branch has passed through.", "It gets steadily harder, with the iteration count climbing without a break from 11 to 36 as the conductance rises.", "It converges throughout, with the iteration count rising to 36 and the reported residual staying below 4.3048e-10 lb/d.", "It refuses at the first conductance that drives the crosslink through zero pressure difference, since the module will not difference across a cusp."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 600 lb/d per root psi the crosslink carries 1884.858788 lb/d across 1.218345e+1 psi and the solve takes 9 iterations for a residual of 2.7285e-12 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 7, 'The crosslink changes sign between loop leg conductances of 300 and 340, and the solve is fine at both. The band that will not converge runs from 360 to 400. Why is the band not centred on the sign change?', '["The band is centred on the cusp after all, and the crossing between 300 and 340 lb/d per root psi is an artefact of the sweep having been printed at far too few conductances for the true centre to show.", "The Jacobian step grows with pressure, so by a loop leg of 360 lb/d per root psi the step has widened far enough to swallow a pressure difference that had been comfortably outside it back at 340 lb/d.", "What breaks is the path rather than one bad entry, because the solver passes near the cusp on the way to the answer and a chord taken while it is passing sends it somewhere it cannot recover from.", "The line search stalls only once the crosslink flow exceeds the loop leg''s own capacity, which happens on the far side of the crossing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those rows sit at pressure differences of one and two psi against a Jacobian step of about 0.007805 psi, so they are hundreds of steps from the cusp.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 8, 'Seven rows of the cusp walk report residuals of tens of pounds a day after as many as 36 iterations. What do those returns look like to a caller?', '["Empty, because the module returns nothing at all but the failure message when a solve has not met its tolerance inside the iteration cap it was given.", "Complete: ok true, with a full set of pressures, flows and well rates, and converged is the only field separating them from the rest.", "Flagged, because the warnings array names the branch that sat nearest to its own cusp and the conductance at which the trouble first started.", "Refused, since a solve that cannot resolve a cusp is the second condition on which the module comes back with ok false."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in the return names a branch''s distance from its own cusp, so the reader has to compute the step, take the branch pressure differences and divide.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 9, 'diagnose reports both a biggest drop and a bottleneck. On what basis does it rank each?', '["The biggest drop on the signed pressure difference and the bottleneck on magnitude, so a backflow branch can never win the first ranking.", "Both on the signed quantities, so a branch running backwards is ranked below every branch running as it was drawn.", "The biggest drop on magnitude and the bottleneck on the signed drop, which is why a backflow is reported separately from the ranking.", "Both on magnitude: the biggest drop on the absolute pressure difference and the intensity on the absolute drop over the absolute mass."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A backflow branch is ranked on the size of its drop and its mass and not on their sign, which is why the crosslink at -1.193210823 psi is ranked with the rest.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 10, 'The two rankings differ in something other than their basis. What is it?', '["Their population: the bottleneck ranks only over branches carrying mass above 1e-9, while the biggest drop ranks over every branch including a dead one.", "Their units, since a drop is in psi and an intensity is in psi per lb/d, so only the second is comparable across networks.", "Their direction, since one takes the largest value and the other takes the smallest.", "Their timing, since one is computed on the last iterate and the other on the audited flows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A dead leg with a large pressure difference across it can therefore be the reported biggest drop and can never be the bottleneck.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 11, 'On AGBADA WEST the biggest drop is the AGBADA-2 flowline at 476.806786 psi and the bottleneck is the AGBADA-9 flowline. Why do the two differ here?', '["The AGBADA-2 flowline runs backwards, so its pressure drop is signed negative and it is excluded from the intensity ranking on exactly that account, leaving the second largest drop to take the title.", "The AGBADA-9 flowline is the only branch on the whole system whose pressure difference sits inside the Jacobian step, so its Jacobian entry is unreliable and the ranking picks it for that reason.", "The AGBADA-9 flowline buys 406.581740456 psi of drop with only 3992.446687538 lb/d, an intensity of 1.018377e-1 against 7.940329e-2 for the branch that spends more pressure moving more mass.", "The trunk carries 12955.677150912 lb/d and is excluded from both rankings, which leaves the two flowlines to split the two titles between them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A trunk carrying everything is supposed to have the biggest drop, and pointing at it every time would be useless, which is the whole design of the function.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 12, 'On the second published diagnose fixture the trunk takes 250.000000 psi carrying 40300.000000 lb/d and the choked flowline takes 150.000000 psi carrying 300.000000 lb/d. What does the function report?', '["The trunk as both, since it takes the larger drop and carries the larger mass and therefore wins on either measure.", "The trunk as the biggest drop and the choked leg as the bottleneck, at intensities of 6.203474e-3 and 5.000000e-1 psi per lb/d.", "The choked leg as both, since a bottleneck ranking that disagreed with the drop would be useless to a reader trying to act on it.", "Neither, because the fixture was built so the two rankings tie and the function then declines to name a branch at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is the case the fixture exists for: the biggest drop and the ranking separate on purpose, and the ranking still picks the intense leg.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 13, 'The intensity carries one guard, at a billionth of a pound a day. What does that guard do, and what does it fail to do?', '["It floors the mass at a billionth of a pound a day, so a whisper leg scores the same intensity as the largest branch on the network.", "It excludes any branch below a billionth of a pound a day from both rankings, which is why a dead leg never appears in either.", "It scales the intensity by the largest branch mass, which makes the ranking relative and therefore comparable between networks.", "It stops a division by zero and nothing else, so a leg carrying almost nothing with one psi across it wins the ranking every time."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Across the whisper sweep the winner never changes over ten orders of magnitude, and a leg carrying 1.00e+2 lb/d is a real flow rate rather than numerical dust.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 14, 'Why is the ranking''s behaviour called a note rather than a defect?', '["Every real case in the digest ranks exactly as the header says, and a relative floor at one part in ten thousand of the largest branch mass would close it and change no ranking here.", "Because the whisper leg is a constructed fixture, and a leg carrying a millionth of a pound a day cannot occur on a solved network.", "Because the ranking is never read by a caller, since diagnose is a reporting function and no field in it feeds the solve.", "Because the independent oracle ranks a bottleneck too and agrees with the engine on every published case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The failure mode is failing open: a ranking with no floor cannot decline, so it names a branch whether or not the branch means anything.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-cusps-and-rankings', 15, 'buildNetwork checks eleven conditions at the door and refuses each with a sentence. What does none of them look at?', '["A pressure, since every refusal is about the mass a node carries rather than about the pressure it sits at.", "A branch, since the eleven refusals are all about nodes and the branches are validated later by the solve itself.", "A callback, so the requirement that decides whether an answer means anything is enforced nowhere.", "A delivery point, which is why a network with no boundary reaches the solver and comes back with ok true."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every one of the eleven is topology or identity: a node with no route to a delivery point, a delivery point with no pressure, a kind that is not one of well, junction, sink.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'You have a solve return in front of you and you are about to quote a pressure from it. What has to be done first?', '["Read converged, because a flag set true is the module''s statement that every field beside it can be quoted as an answer.", "Run the audit yourself and write down produced, delivered, gap and relative, because the solver never runs it for you.", "Read the reported residual and treat it as the uncertainty on every pressure in the object, since it is the only error figure the module produces.", "Re-solve from a second starting guess and check that the pressures agree, since two runs landing together is what confirms a network answer."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in the solve return is a check that was not computed by the iteration it would be checking, and on the teaching network the audit turns a residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d into a gap of 345.000000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'A node is named in pinned. What two questions does the reading order tell you to ask about it?', '["What its branch conductance is, and how many Jacobian steps its branch sits from zero pressure difference.", "Whether it is a well, a junction or a sink, and which of the eleven build refusals it came closest to triggering.", "How many iterations the solve took, and whether the warning naming it appeared before or after the failure message.", "What its own nodal imbalance is, and where its starting pressure was."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine carries that imbalance where nothing consults it, and the starting pressure is what decided the entry, so a pinned node is not a solved pressure however it is printed.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'The warning on a pinned node tells one story. Why does the reading order say to treat pinned as a verdict rather than as that story?', '["A producing well on a live line gets the same sentence as a shut-in well on a dead line, so the sentence carries no information about which case you have.", "The warning is written by buildNetwork rather than by the solve itself, so what it describes is the drawing that was handed in and not the answer that came back.", "The warning appears only when converged comes back false, so on a converged answer it has already been suppressed.", "The warning names the node but not the branch, so it cannot say which side of the network lost the mass."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first clause of the sentence is a fact about the Jacobian row and is true whenever the warning fires, and the second is a story about the field the module has no evidence for.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'The reading order says to convert the tolerance before trusting it. What has to be computed, and from what?', '["The residual in relative form, by dividing the reported residual by the total mass that the whole network delivers at its boundary.", "The tolerance restated in psi, by dividing the tolerance that was asked for by the largest branch conductance anywhere on the system.", "The scale, which is the largest single well inflow at the delivery pressure, then the target as the tolerance times that scale.", "The target from the returned object, which names the scale it used alongside the residual it stopped at."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the teaching network the scale is 7883.717950413 lb/d, so the documented default of 0.000001 is a target of 7.883718e-3 lb/d and the return names neither figure.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'Why does the reading order put branch directions before any stream work?', '["The propagation refuses a network with a backflow, so the signs decide whether a stream can be walked at all.", "flows is signed along the drawn sense while a branch stream mass runs along the solved sense and is always positive.", "A stream split at a junction is by pressure share, and a sign error in a branch flow reverses the pressure difference it is taken from.", "The directions are what the drawing asserts and the streams are what the solve found, so taking them in that order preserves the drawing''s intent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A drawn arrow is a guess made before anybody solved anything, and taking it at face value gets the sign, the split and the whole downstream reading wrong.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'Why does the reading order put the bottleneck and the biggest drop last, and report them separately?', '["They are different questions: one asks which branch eats the most pressure for what it carries and the other asks only which drop is largest.", "They are computed from the audited flows rather than from the solve return, so neither of them can be read until the conservation audit has been run by hand.", "They are the two fields most likely to disagree with the oracle, which ranks a bottleneck of its own on every published case.", "They are the only two fields in the result whose units are arbitrary, so they have to be read after everything carrying a real unit."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A trunk carrying everything is supposed to have the biggest drop, and a ranking that pointed at it every time would be useless.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'Which pressures in a network answer are determined rather than iterated?', '["Every pressure the solve reports converged on, since convergence is the module''s statement that a pressure has stopped moving between iterations.", "Every unpinned pressure, since reversing the nodes array moves those by no more than 1.1369e-13 psia.", "A boundary pressure, which is an input, and the pressures a closed form produced on a network whose branch relations were linear.", "Every well pressure, since a Vogel inflow fixes one once the header it pushes against has been solved."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the published linear_star the closed form and Newton agree at 546.666666667 psia exactly and at the other nodes to -5.6843e-14 and -2.8422e-14 psia, relatives of 1.4330e-16 and 1.1269e-16.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'What makes a number a last iterate rather than a determined one, and how good can a last iterate be?', '["A tolerance looser than 1e-12 is what makes a pressure an iterate, and asking for a tighter one turns that same pressure back into a determined number.", "A pinned node makes every pressure on the network an iterate, since the undetermined entry propagates into all of them through the branch attached to it.", "An iteration count above 2 makes a pressure an iterate, because Newton lands on a determined answer in a single step and confirms it in a second.", "A turbulent branch anywhere in the network makes every pressure an iterate, and a good one is reached by two unrelated methods and is still not exact."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the published turbulent_tree the engine lands within -3.9037e-9 psia of the bisection oracle at one header, taking 6 Newton steps against 48 oracle sweeps.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'Which entries in a network answer are neither determined nor a last iterate?', '["The branch flows, since a flow is derived from two pressures and inherits the error of both.", "A pinned node''s pressure, which comes back at 400.000000, 600.000000, 831.000000, 1182.577035 or 2000.000000 psia depending only on where the solve started.", "The well rates, since an inflow relation is a callback the module never evaluates for itself.", "The reported residual, since it is a measurement of the solve rather than a property of the network."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those five starts give gaps of 1625.000000, 1625.000000, 345.000000, 0.000000 and -640.000000 lb/d, and every one reports converged.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'What does the independent bisection oracle establish, and where does it stop?', '["It publishes 4 clean cases and records no defects at all, and it has no concept of a pinned node, converges on how far pressures moved rather than on a mass residual, and never calls the audit, the ranking or the propagation.", "It reproduces every field of the engine''s return on 4 cases, which is what makes the engine''s convergence report trustworthy in general.", "It fails on the teaching network, which is how the pinned node and its 345.000000000 lb/d were found in the first place.", "It takes 19, 42 and 48 sweeps against a cap of 4000 it never reaches, and its published gaps are what the engine''s residual is held to."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every finding in this tier lives where the referee never looks, so the tier has to establish each one against the engine itself.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'Which of these does the module not model at all, so that no number in the course speaks to it?', '["A loop, since the split between two parallel paths has no closed form at all and the module is written to solve trees of branches and nothing else.", "A backflow, since a branch is walked along its drawn direction and a negative flow is reported as a refusal.", "Temperature anywhere, so no thermal coupling and no cooldown, and every equation is steady state so no slugging, no holdup and no transient.", "A delivery point, whose pressure the caller supplies and which the module therefore treats as outside the system."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mass in equals mass out on every branch by construction, so there is no compressibility along a branch either, and the pipe hydraulics and the well inflow are both callbacks the consumer supplies.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'A gathering system has a compressor between two junctions. How does this module take it?', '["It is a fourth node kind the module accepts, alongside well, junction and sink, with its discharge pressure supplied as an input.", "It is a delivery point with a fixed pressure, which is the standard way a module with three node kinds represents any pressure raising equipment.", "It is a well with a negative inflow, which is how the module represents anything that puts pressure into the system rather than taking it out.", "It does not. There are three node kinds, so anything else has to be written as a branch relation or left out."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A well injects and its pressure is unknown, a junction injects nothing and everything that arrives leaves, and a sink has a fixed pressure and takes whatever arrives.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'What does solving the whole system at once earn that no single-well method can produce?', '["A tighter answer on each well, since the network solve carries a residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d and a solo study carries none at all.", "The rate each well makes falls out of the answer rather than out of a wellhead pressure somebody typed in, and being on the header costs 2382.375141650 lb/d here, 15.190761959 percent.", "The pipe hydraulics along each flowline, which a single-well study has to assume and the network solve computes from the pipe table.", "A conservation gap for the system, which is the one figure a single-well study can never form because it has only one well in it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The four solo rates add to 15683.052292561 lb/d while the system produces 13300.677150912 lb/d, and neither column can be got at from the other by any single-well method.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'Ranking the four teaching wells by what the network costs them gives a different order from ranking them by what they make. Why?', '["The percentage is taken against the solo rate and the rate is taken against the system rate, so the two rankings are computed over different denominators and cannot agree.", "One well is pinned and reports a loss of zero, which removes it from the loss ranking and shifts every other well by one place.", "A well with little margin over the header has the least to give when the header rises, so AGBADA-6 loses 24.162893177 percent of itself while the strongest well loses 12.857585591.", "The trunk carries every well''s mass, so a well far from the separator loses more to friction and the loss ranking is really a distance ranking."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The loss ranking is the physics of margin over a rising header, and the rate ranking is the one every allocation meeting uses.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'One habit sits under everything in this tier. What is it?', '["Asking of every returned number what would have had to be true for the function to refuse instead of returning it.", "Asking whether the reported residual is small enough for the decision that the number in front of you is about to be used in.", "Asking whether the answer agrees with a second run started from a different initial guess before it is quoted to anybody.", "Asking which of the two modules owns the number, since only one of the two iterates and the other cannot be wrong."]'::jsonb, 0, 'buildNetwork refuses eleven malformed networks with a reason, and solveNetwork refuses only a singular Jacobian, so the question sorts what was checked from what was merely returned.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'A script reads pressures, flows and wellRates out of a solve on AGBADA WEST and stops there. What does it get?', '["A result with the allocated well''s rate already corrected to the 640.000000000 lb/d its line can pass, since the module writes the pin into the flows before returning.", "An ordinary answer with no mark on it anywhere, because the pin does not zero the reported rate, does not adjust the flowline and does not appear in flows.", "A result missing one node, since a pinned node is dropped from the returned pressures along with its row and its column.", "A result whose trunk flow carries a warning marker on it, which is the one place the pin is recorded outside the warnings array."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The dropped part is one row and one column of the linear system, and the kept part is every field a caller reads.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'A careful caller works out the scale on this network, multiplies by the tolerance to get the target, and finds the reported residual comfortably below it. What has that established?', '["That the answer is good to the target in lb/d, which is what the scaled criterion was designed to guarantee on a system of any size.", "That every nodal imbalance on the network is below the target, since the residual is a maximum over the unknowns and a maximum bounds its members.", "That the solve did not hit its iteration cap, since a capped solve reports a residual above the target by definition.", "That the iteration stopped moving, and nothing about whether the mass balanced, because the norm the residual comes from had the pinned node filtered out of it before the comparison was made."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The target on this network at 1e-12 is 7.883718e-9 lb/d and the audit on the same answer reports 345.000000000 lb/d, so meeting the criterion and balancing the mass are different statements.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'The worst nodal imbalance over all seven unknowns on this network is 345.000000000 lb/d. What does the engine report as residualLbD?', '["1.546141e-11 lb/d, the worst over the unpinned nodes only.", "345.000000000 lb/d, since the imbalance object and the residual are built from the same array of unknowns.", "3.450000e+2 lb/d divided by the scale of 7883.717950413 lb/d, which is how a relative criterion reports an absolute imbalance.", "9.094947e-13 lb/d, the worst over the well nodes, since a junction imbalance is a bookkeeping quantity rather than a physical one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Six of the seven unknowns sit at machine noise and the seventh is the pinned one, which was filtered out before the maximum was taken.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Run the audit across eight solved cases and six of them come back at machine noise. What do the other two have in common?', '["Each ran a turbulent branch, since a turbulent relation has no closed form and its answer is a last iterate rather than an exact one.", "Each was solved at the module default tolerance rather than at 1e-12, so each stopped one Newton step earlier than it needed to.", "Each had a node pinned, and each reports converged true with a residual no larger than 1.546141e-11 lb/d.", "Each is a teaching case rather than a published golden, so neither has ever been checked by the independent bisection referee."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reading down the gap column separates the answers and reading down the residual column does not, and the two that are not noise report 3.450000e+2 and 1000.000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'wellStreams[id].massLbD and wellRates[id] sit in one result and both are a mass in lb/d. What is the difference between them?', '["One is signed along the drawn direction and the other along the solved direction, so they differ only where a branch runs backwards.", "One was typed in by whoever ran the well test and one came out of the iteration, and nothing in the module compares the two.", "One is the mass at the wellhead and the other the mass at the node, and the two differ by whatever the flowline holds up.", "One is a last iterate and the other is determined, so the second is the number a caller should quote in every case."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One comparison at the top of the propagation would catch a caller error and a solver hole at once, and the module makes none.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'One row of the flows against branchStreams table shows a difference of exactly twice what the branch carries. What does that ratio identify?', '["A single mass written twice under two conventions, which is the crosslink at 1179.729250341 lb/d against the 589.864625170 lb/d it carries.", "A branch counted at both of its ends, which is what happens when a junction carrying three branches on it is walked twice by the propagation.", "A mass that is simply wrong by a factor of two, which is one row of the multiplier sweep left in the table by mistake.", "A branch whose stream was propagated before the solve had converged, so it carries the mass of the previous iterate as well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A mass that is simply wrong does not double. The factor of two is the fingerprint of a sign convention, and no other branch on this network has it.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'The number 345.000000000 lb/d turns up as the conservation gap, as the pinned node''s own imbalance, and on three branches of the stream table. Is that one finding or several?', '["Three separate findings that happen to share a value, since a conservation gap, a nodal imbalance and a stream disagreement are computed by three functions from three different inputs.", "One finding reported three times by design, which is the module making a hole visible in every part of the result it touches.", "One quantity seen in three places: a well allocated 985 lb/d on a line passing 640 lb/d, whose shortfall the audit totals, the imbalance object holds and the propagation carries downstream.", "Two findings, since the gap and the imbalance are the solve''s and the three stream rows are the sign convention showing up again."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The propagation takes the reported rate at face value, so the shortfall enters at that well''s own flowline and rides unchanged through the north bypass and the trunk.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'The separator on this network is told oil, water, gas and a mass. Which of those four is not an answer to a network problem?', '["The gas, because a gas rate in Mscf/d cannot be added across two pressures the way a mass rate in lb/d can be added along a branch.", "The water, because the trunk water cut of 33.262507244 percent is a ratio and a ratio is never conserved at a junction.", "None of the four, because the propagation is exact arithmetic and every quantity it returns inherits that exactness.", "The mass, because the component rates are the well tests added along the solved directions and the mass beside them was handed in."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oil 3455.000000000 stb/d, water 1722.000000000 stb/d and gas 3536.000000000 Mscf/d are the sums of the four tests to the last digit, and the mass carries a 345.000000000 lb/d shortfall.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Why is the linear case the only place in this course where the solver can be checked with no tolerance at all?', '["The linear branch relation is differentiable at zero pressure difference, so the differenced Jacobian is exact and the residual comes back at exactly zero.", "The system collapses to a weighted graph Laplacian whose solution is a matrix inverse, and Newton and Gaussian elimination share no code and no reasoning.", "The published golden was generated by the closed form itself, so the two routes are guaranteed to agree by construction.", "The oracle solves the linear case by a route of its own, which gives three independent answers rather than two."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nobody chose a tolerance to make two nodes agree exactly and two agree to the last bits of a double, and the gate holds the pair at a relative 1e-12, which is loose against what they deliver.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Of seven runs of the same network, one comes back with a conservation gap of 0.000000 lb/d. What has that run found?', '["A cancellation, since two errors of opposite sign in a loop can leave a total that closes without any node being right.", "A shut-in, since the only way a network with an over allocated well can balance is for that well to stop producing.", "The answer: the pinned node at 1182.577035 psia, where this well''s Vogel inflow equals the 640.000000 lb/d its line can pass.", "A tighter tolerance, since a gap of zero is what a solve reports once it has been asked for more digits than the default supplies."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The other six rows are mass imbalanced and none of them is a solution, and every one of the seven reports converged true.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'The engine default puts every unknown at the separator pressure, and the flat top of this well''s allocation runs from zero up to 1013.848652 psia. What is the consequence?', '["The default starts inside the flat top on a node whose Jacobian row is already flat, so it converges to a non-solution and reports converged true.", "The default starts below the module pressure floor of 14.7 psia, so the very first Newton step has to climb out of it before it can do anything useful.", "The default starts at the one pressure in the problem that is not an unknown, so the solve is exact on its first step and refines from there onward.", "The default starts above the flat top, so the node is live at the outset and only becomes flat once the line saturates."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A correct answer exists and the solver reaches it from a 1200 psia start in 7 iterations, so this is a guess starting inside a trap rather than a hard problem.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Two runs of this network report a residual of 2.2737e-12 lb/d and a gap of 1625.000000 lb/d. A third reports 1.5461e-11 lb/d and 345.000000 lb/d. What should a caller conclude about ranking runs by residual?', '["That the ranking is sound but needs the scale applied first, since a residual is absolute and the criterion that produced it was relative.", "That the ranking is sound and the two runs at 2.2737e-12 lb/d are the ones to keep, since a smaller residual is a smaller nodal imbalance.", "That the ranking would work if the runs were compared at equal iteration counts, since all three took 7 iterations except the default.", "That the ranking is worthless here, because the reported residual improves as the answer worsens and the criterion behind it was a scaled target the return never names."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing in the return names the scale, the target or which test stopped the loop, and on this sweep the residual is measured over a set of nodes that excludes the one carrying the error.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'Four wells opened straight onto the separator pressure would make 7883.717950413, 3125.903030303, 5561.824807605 and 985.000000000 lb/d. Which of those figures becomes the scale?', '["Their total, 17556.445788322 lb/d, since the criterion has to be relative to the whole system it is holding.", "The largest, 7883.717950413 lb/d, and the other three are not used at all.", "Their mean, since a maximum norm over the unknowns is compared against an average of what the wells can put in.", "The smallest, 985.000000000 lb/d, so the criterion is never looser than the weakest well on the system could justify."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That choice is what makes the effective criterion tighten as wells are added, which is the opposite of what a relative scale is for.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'A gathering system grows from one well to three. What happens to the standard the solve is held to?', '["It loosens, because each new well adds inflow and the scale is built from the total the wells put in.", "It stays fixed, because the scale is a property of the delivery pressure rather than of the wells attached to the system.", "It tightens, because the total inflow at the sink grows while the scale keeps only the largest single well.", "It moves either way, because the scale takes whichever well is nearest the separator and that is not the largest one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the published ladder the ratio of total inflow to scale runs 1.000000000, 1.687637489 and 2.385868167, and the scale itself only moves when a bigger well arrives.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Loosen the tolerance on this network to 1.0e-1 and the solve still reports converged after 6 iterations. What moved?', '["The worst junction pressure by 1.7153e+1 psi and the crosslink by 2.4182e+2 lb/d, which is a change of answer and not a change of stopping point.", "Nothing, because a tolerance decides when the iteration stops and never what the answer is.", "Only the reported residual, which rises to 4.6571e+2 lb/d while every pressure and every flow stays exactly where it was.", "The scale, which is rebuilt from the wells whenever the tolerance changes, so the target moves along with it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two pairs of rows in that sweep do repeat their answer exactly, which is what tempts a reader into thinking the criterion never touches the answer.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'A branch relation that is continuous and monotone satisfies the module contract. Why is that not enough?', '["A monotone relation can be flat over an interval, and a flat interval is what pins a node and takes it out of the measurement.", "The Jacobian is differenced rather than differentiated, and a difference approximates a slope, which the contract never asked for.", "The contract is stated on pTo alone, so a relation monotone in the pressure difference can still be non monotone in either end pressure.", "Continuity is checked at the door and monotonicity is not, so a relation can pass the build and fail inside the solve."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The turbulent law is continuous everywhere and monotone everywhere and its slope at zero pressure difference is infinite, which is the case the contract as written invites.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'Over a band of loop leg conductances the solve runs as many as 36 iterations, ends at a residual of tens of pounds a day, and comes back with a complete set of pressures, flows and well rates. What is ok?', '["False, since an unresolved cusp is one of the three conditions the module refuses on.", "True on the rows that converged and false on the rows that did not, which is how converged and ok are kept consistent.", "Absent, since the module omits ok from a return that did not meet its tolerance.", "True, on every row of that band."]'::jsonb, 3, 'converged is the only field separating those rows from the rest, and nothing in the return names how close a branch sits to its own cusp.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'Across the band of loop leg conductances where this network stops converging, the reported residual runs from 4.5926e+1 down to 2.2405e+1 lb/d. How does that stand against the criterion the solve was given?', '["Below it, since the target is the tolerance multiplied by a scale of 7883.717950413 lb/d and a scaled target is looser than the tolerance asked for by exactly that factor, which is what admits these rows.", "Not comparable, because the residual in a return that did not converge is the last value the line search saw rather than a nodal imbalance at all.", "Nine orders above the target of 7.883718e-9 lb/d, and the return still comes back ok true with a full set of pressures.", "Exactly at it, which is why converged flips to false rather than the solve running on to the module cap of 200 iterations."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The residual falls as the conductance rises through that band while the solve gets no better, so neither the flag nor the figure is telling the caller what to do.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'The reading order puts pinned ahead of converged. Why in that order?', '["A non-empty pinned list means the number converged was tested against is not a measurement of the whole network, and that stays true whatever the residual says.", "converged is set before the pin is decided, so reading it first gives you a flag from an earlier stage of the same call.", "pinned is populated only on a converged return, so converged has to be read first to know whether pinned is worth reading.", "converged is a solver flag and pinned is a physical statement, and a physical statement outranks a numerical one in any reading order."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On this network converged is true at a residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d while the audit reports 345.000000000 lb/d, because the one node that was not converged had been excluded from the norm.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'A study needs the cooldown time of a shut in flowline. What does this module contribute?', '["The steady state pressure profile along the line, which is the boundary condition a cooldown calculation is started from.", "Nothing. There is no temperature anywhere in it, so no thermal coupling and no cooldown.", "The mass held in the line at shut in, which the module computes from the branch flows and the pipe bore.", "The wall thickness and the grade of the line, which the pipe table publishes and which set how fast the line loses heat."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every equation here is steady state, so there is no slugging, no holdup and no transient of any kind, and mass in equals mass out on every branch by construction.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Two different things on this course make a branch flow come back negative. What are they, and are they the same kind of finding?', '["A sign convention in branchStreams and a sign convention in flows, which are two spellings of one bookkeeping choice.", "A recirculating loop and a dead leg, both of which the propagation refuses with a message naming the branch at fault.", "A crosslink that genuinely runs against its drawn arrow at the solution, and a capacity limited flowline left running backwards by a starting guess, and only the first is a property of the answer.", "A backflow that diagnose names and a backflow it does not, the difference being whether the branch carries mass above the guard of 1e-9."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The crosslink at -589.864625170 lb/d is what the solve found on a determined part of the network. The -640.000000 lb/d flowline on the low starting guesses is an artefact of where a pinned node was left.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'One run of this network reaches the correct answer in 7 iterations and another burns 36 and does not converge. Is an iteration count a measure of answer quality?', '["Yes, on a linear network only, where Newton is exact and 2 iterations is precisely what an exact method needs in order to land and then confirm.", "No. A low count can sit on a non-solution and a high one on a branch near its own cusp, and neither count says anything about the mass balance.", "Yes, because the count is the one field that separates a converged solve from one that hit its cap.", "No, because the count depends on the nodes array order, which a reordering moves by 5.4710e-9 psia at the pinned node."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The default guess converges in 11 iterations to an answer 345.000000000 lb/d out of balance, and the cusp walk climbs to 36 iterations with ok still true.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'A solve hits its iteration cap and another sits on a cusp it cannot resolve. Compare the two returns.', '["Both come back ok true with a complete set of pressures, flows and well rates, and converged false, so the object''s shape says nothing about which failure you are holding.", "The capped solve returns ok false and the cusp returns ok true, since only one of the two is a condition the module recognises.", "Both come back converged true, because the module sets converged from the line search rather than from the residual.", "The capped solve carries a failure message and the cusp carries a warning naming the branch, which is how the two are told apart."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The only failure that comes back ok false is a singular Jacobian, and its message is a diagnosis about nodes moving together rather than a repair.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'On one answer the engine''s warning says a dead line and diagnose reports dead legs as none. Which is right, and what does the disagreement show?', '["The warning is right, because it is written from the Jacobian row and diagnose only looks at branches carrying mass above the guard of 1e-9.", "Both are right, because they use different definitions of a dead leg and the module documents each in its own header.", "Neither can be right, because the branch in question carries 640.000000000 lb/d and that is neither dead nor live by the module''s own test.", "diagnose is right, and the warning is a sentence asserting a diagnosis nothing checked."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The well is producing 985.000000000 lb/d and its flowline is passing 640.000000000 lb/d, so the module''s reading function and its warning contradict each other and only one of them looked.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'propagateStreams returns ok true on a mass doubled by the caller, and solveNetwork returns ok true at its iteration cap. What is the lesson about that field?', '["ok reports whether the call completed without an exception, which both of those calls did, so both returns are correct and the field is being misread.", "buildNetwork trained the caller to key on ok by refusing eleven networks with it, and neither of the other two functions honours the habit.", "ok is set from converged in both functions, so a caller who reads either one of them has read both.", "ok is reserved for topology, so a caller should read warnings instead, which is populated on both of those returns."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The one thing that does come back ok false out of the solve is a singular Jacobian, and the propagation refuses only a recirculating set of solved directions.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'Reversing the nodes array moves one pressure by 5.4710e-9 psia and changing the starting guess moves the same pressure by hundreds of psia. What sees either of those?', '["The residual sees both of them, which is exactly why it moves from 1.5461e-11 lb/d down to 2.2737e-12 lb/d across the whole sweep of starting guesses.", "The warnings array sees both of them, since a pinned node is named on every one of those runs and the sentence that names it also carries its pressure.", "Neither is visible in the residual, which is a maximum taken over the nodes that exclude this one, so only the audit changes when the answer does.", "The iteration count sees both, falling from 11 to 7 as the starting guess is moved away from the separator pressure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unpinned nodes move by at most 1.1369e-13 psia under a reordering, so the solved part is reproducible and the pinned entry is a different kind of number wearing the same label.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Loosening the tolerance from 1.0e-10 to 1.0e-8 leaves the answer identical. Doubling every well stream mass changes the trunk stream by a factor of two. What separates those two sweeps?', '["One varies a stopping rule and the answer had already passed the target, and the other varies an input the module carries without ever checking it against the solve.", "One is run on the solve and the other on the propagation, and the propagation is exact arithmetic so any change to it must show in the output.", "One changes the scaled target and the other changes the scale, and only the second can move a mass anywhere in the result.", "One is a published sweep and the other is a derived one, so only the first has been checked against the independent referee."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The solved trunk stays at 12955.677151 lb/d on every row of the multiplier sweep, and the stream trunk runs from 10640.541721 to 26601.354302 lb/d with ok true and no warnings.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'diagnose ranks the branches of this network and names a bottleneck at an intensity of 1.018377e-1. What is that ranking computed from?', '["The branch stream masses, which are always positive, so the ranking is unaffected by the branch that runs backwards.", "The audited flows, which is why diagnose has to be called after checkConservation rather than before it.", "The nodal imbalances, since a bottleneck is a node level quantity expressed on the branch that causes it.", "Flows that do not balance, and no part of the reading knows it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The answer those intensities come from reports 13300.677150912 lb/d produced against 12955.677150912 lb/d delivered, a gap of 345.000000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'The same topology as the pinning fixture, but with a real branch and a real inflow, returns a wellhead of 153.846153846 psia and a rate of 16153.846153843 lb/d. What does that run establish?', '["That the fixture''s own numbers were badly chosen, since one topology cannot carry a constant 2000.000000 lb/d inflow and a real one, and the gate asserts both on the same shape of network.", "That a live network reports pinned as none and raises no warning, which is what makes the flag readable when it does appear.", "That the pin is a complaint about conditioning, since only the constant version of the case is badly enough scaled to raise it.", "That the module repairs a pinnable node wherever it can and pins one only where no repair is available to it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pinned flag that fired on every network would carry no information, and the two cases differ only in whether anything depends on the node''s pressure.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Under what condition does two methods agreeing on an answer count as evidence?', '["When both were run at the same tolerance, since a difference in the stopping criterion is the usual reason two methods land apart from each other.", "When both report converged, since a converged flag arriving from two separate routes is a stronger statement than one arriving from either alone.", "When they had no way to agree by accident, which is why a closed form against Newton says something and two runs of one solver do not.", "When one of them is the published golden, since a golden is committed by an independent referee and is therefore the reference."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two runs of the same solver share the assembly, the Jacobian, the convergence test and the filter, so they agree everywhere the shared machinery is wrong.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'A caller wants to know which criterion actually stopped their solve. What does the returned object give them toward it?', '["An absolute residual in lb/d and nothing else, so the scale has to be rebuilt by evaluating every well inflow at the sink pressure again.", "The scale and the target, both of which are returned in the object alongside the residual for exactly this reason and in the very same units.", "The number of iterations, from which the target can be recovered because the solver halves its own target on every single pass through the loop.", "The tolerance that was asked for, which is the criterion in force whenever a caller supplies one explicitly."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two spellings of one criterion sit in the same function, the first test before the loop against the raw tolerance and every test inside it against the scaled target.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'The module header states one requirement on the well inflow and one on the branch relation. What do the two have in common?', '["Both are checked by buildNetwork before the solve begins, which is why the eleven refusals include a callback that fails to be monotone across the pressure range it will be evaluated over.", "Both are statements about the branches rather than about the nodes, which is why a well carrying a flat topped inflow passes the contract and a flat branch does not.", "Both are enforced by the line search, which halves its step until the relation behaves.", "One shape of failure: a defensible relation satisfies the sentence and breaks the method, and neither requirement is checked at the door."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A well held to an allocation is monotone non increasing with a flat top, and the turbulent law is continuous and monotone with an infinite slope at zero pressure difference.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Sort the entries of pressures into determined, last iterate and neither. Which field predicts the third group before you look at anything else?', '["converged, which comes back false whenever an entry of the third kind is present in the result.", "pinned, which names them, and says nothing at all about how far out of place they are.", "residualLbD, whose size tells you how many entries were left undetermined by the solve.", "warnings, whose length is one sentence per undetermined entry, so counting them counts the third group."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pinned node''s pressure comes back at 400.000000, 600.000000, 831.000000, 1182.577035 or 2000.000000 psia depending only on where the solve started, and every one of those runs reports converged.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'An engineer raises an allocation from 640 lb/d to 660 lb/d and reruns. What is the honest description of what happened?', '["The solve got harder, which the reported residual records by rising from 1.9133e-10 to 6.5211e-10 lb/d.", "The line saturated and the surplus was held at the manifold, which the trunk flow records as a drop of 20.000000 lb/d.", "The node changed kind, from a live node the solver moves to a pinned one it does not, and a 20.000000 lb/d hole opened that no returned field displays.", "Nothing changed, since the flowline was already at its capacity of 640.000000 lb/d and the extra allocation had nowhere to go."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every row of that sweep reports converged true and the reported rate on every row is the allocation, so the crossing is not a change in difficulty.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Someone reconciles a facility measurement against the trunk stream mass and finds it matches. What have they checked?', '["The arithmetic of the well tests they handed in, since the trunk stream is 13300.677150912 lb/d and the trunk the solve found is 12955.677150912 lb/d.", "The solve, since a stream mass is propagated along the solved directions and could not match without them being right.", "Nothing at all, since a stream mass is unsigned and a facility measurement is signed, so the two are not comparable.", "The conservation of the network, since produced against delivered is what a facility measurement stands in for."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no multiplier on this network at which the two masses agree, because the caller handing in the truth is not the same thing as the solve delivering it.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'This well is allocated 985 lb/d, its Vogel curve peaks at 2100 lb/d at a reservoir pressure of 1450 psia, and its line passes 640 lb/d. Where does the flat top end and where does the balance close?', '["The flat top ends at 1182.577035 psia and the balance closes at 1450 psia, where the inflow reaches the reservoir pressure.", "The flat top ends at 1013.848652 psia and the balance closes at 1182.577035 psia.", "The flat top ends at 640 lb/d of inflow and the balance closes at 985 lb/d, since both limits are stated as rates rather than as pressures.", "The flat top ends at 831.176262 psia, which is where the default run leaves the node, and the balance closes at the same pressure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Below 1013.848652 psia the allocation still binds, so a run left there reports 985.000000 lb/d into a 640.000000 lb/d line and loses 345.000000 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'The module writes one failure sentence that is a genuine diagnosis rather than a story. Which is it?', '["The pinning warning, which says the node carried nothing and that nothing depended on its pressure, so it was left where it sits.", "The iteration cap message, which says how many iterations were run and how large the worst nodal imbalance was when they ran out.", "The recirculating message, which says the solved flow directions form a loop and a gathering system does not do that.", "The singular Jacobian message, which says two or more nodes move together so their pressures are not separately determined."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It goes on to say that this is usually a branch connected differently from the way the drawing suggests, and it is the one thing that comes back ok false out of the solve.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'The eight branches of this network sit between 152.883678 and 54991.975344 Jacobian steps from zero pressure difference. What does that spread tell you?', '["That the branch sitting at 152.883678 steps is inside the step, so its Jacobian entry has to be a chord across a square root rather than a slope.", "That the branch at 54991.975344 steps is the one costing this solve its iterations, since a large pressure drop is the expensive thing to resolve.", "That none is in danger and one is worth a second look, on geometry, since the nearest is two orders of magnitude closer than anything else.", "That the spread is meaningless, since the step is recomputed at every iteration and these are distances at the answer only."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The step is max(1e-3, |p| * 1e-5), about 0.007805 psi at these junction pressures, and the reader has to compute it and divide because no field reports it.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'The reading order starts at the build rather than at the solve. What is taken off the build, and why there?', '["The node, branch, unknown pressure and delivery point counts, because every later index in the result is in that order and a refusal at this stage names the node or branch at fault.", "The starting pressures, because the build is where the default guess is written and the solve inherits it unchanged.", "The scale, because the build already has every well inflow and the delivery pressure in front of it.", "Nothing, because the build only validates and the same counts can be read off the solve return instead."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On the teaching network the build returns ok true with nodes 8, branches 8, unknown pressures 7 and delivery points 1, and a valid network is indexed rather than copied.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'What can a network solve tell an allocation meeting that no amount of single-well work can?', '["What each well''s own inflow curve is, since the solve fits one to every well from the rates and the wellhead pressures that it converges on.", "What being on the header costs each well, 2382.375141650 lb/d across these four, which is 15.190761959 percent of what they make alone.", "What the flowline pressure drop is, since the module computes it from the pipe table given the bore and the friction factor.", "What each well would make at a chosen wellhead pressure, since that is the quantity the network solve reports and a solo study assumes."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every single-well study is run against a wellhead pressure somebody typed in, and the four solo rates here add to 15683.052292561 lb/d against a system producing 13300.677150912 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'A pinned pressure is wrong and the audit is the only thing that catches it. Is a nonzero gap therefore proof that a pinned node is misplaced?', '["No, because a gap can equally come from a sign error in the assembly, and the audit cannot tell the two apart on any network.", "No, because the gap is a whole system figure and the pinned node is one entry, so the two are not connected by anything the module computes.", "Yes on any network, because the audit''s own header calls it the only check that catches a sign error and a pinned node is a sign error by another name.", "On this network yes, because the node has one correct pressure and a gap of 0.000000 lb/d is what the right answer looks like."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reading pinned as a note that a node did not matter is the mistake: it marks a pressure the solver stopped deciding, not one the physics stopped deciding.'),
  ('network', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Two of the quantities this module works with are supplied by the consumer as callbacks. Which two, and what follows?', '["The branch conductances and the delivery pressure, so a network can be solved without either being known in advance, which is the property that makes the module reusable.", "The stream masses and the component rates, which is why the propagation carries a mass it has no reason to trust and returns ok true whatever it is handed.", "The pipe hydraulics and the well inflow, and the module states a requirement on each and checks neither.", "The Jacobian step and the iteration cap, which the caller supplies because only the caller knows how near a branch sits to its own cusp."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in this module computes a pressure drop along a pipe or a rate out of a reservoir, so the two requirements that decide whether an answer means anything are enforced nowhere.');
